    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002000 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002000">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#topObjectProperty"/>
        <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000067"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A modifier of a clinical association. Scope-neutral grouping property: annotation should assert through the sub-property that commits to a scope, and this property is used for querying across scopes or for data from a source that did not record scope.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Analogous to RO:0000053 (has characteristic) in that the association bears the qualifier, but not declared as a sub-property because BFO type constraints require the bearer to be an independent continuant and the characteristic to be a specifically dependent continuant. CLCO associations are information content entities and qualifiers are not modeled as BFO dependent continuants.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Renamed from "has association qualifier" on 2026-10-20, when qualifier scope was moved onto the property. This is now the scope-neutral parent; its former label was transferred to CLCO:0003000, which is the statement-scoped child.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002000</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has qualifier</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053"/>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

Use this property only when the scope of the qualifier is genuinely unknown or deliberately left
open, for example in pre-adjudication annotation or when importing data that did not record scope.

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:

"A soft-tissue tumor was suspected."
Use: has association qualifier, NOT the grouping property has qualifier. The uncertainty applies to
the whole statement.

"Possible pneumonia was treated with antibiotics."
Use: has subject qualifier, NOT the grouping property has qualifier. The treatment happened; only
the diagnosis is uncertain.

"The patient reported pain in his left knee."
Use: has object qualifier, NOT the grouping property has qualifier.

Every sub-property entails this one, so a query on has qualifier retrieves qualifiers at any scope.
Annotation should always commit to a scope.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002002 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002002">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000060"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its intention qualifier, indicating that the association is intended rather than actual.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002002</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has intention</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"The patient will start chemotherapy."
SOURCE: "chemotherapy" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "will start" =&gt; intention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has intention =&gt; TARGET

"The patient intended to undergo surgery."
SOURCE: "surgery" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "intended" =&gt; intention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has intention =&gt; TARGET

NEGATIVE EXAMPLE:

"The patient underwent surgery."
"underwent" describes an actual event, NOT intention.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002003 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002003">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000062"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its recommendation qualifier, indicating that the association represents a clinical recommendation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002003</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has recommended</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"The physician recommended an MRI."
SOURCE: "MRI" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "recommended" =&gt; recommended
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has recommended =&gt; TARGET

"The patient should follow up in two weeks."
SOURCE: "follow up" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "should" =&gt; recommended
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has recommended =&gt; TARGET

NEGATIVE EXAMPLE:

"The patient underwent an MRI."
"underwent" describes an actual intervention, NOT a recommendation.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002004 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002004">
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its negation status, indicating whether the asserted relationship is negated.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Obsoleted 2026-10-20. Named the axis but not the scope. Because negation (CLCO:0000080) applies at any scope, an assertion through this property discarded the scope, and the same annotation could legitimately be spelled three different ways. The axis is already carried by the value class, so attach negation through the property that commits to a scope.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:consider>CLCO:0003000, CLCO:0003001, CLCO:0003002</oboInOwl:consider>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002004</oboInOwl:id>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
        <rdfs:label>obsolete has negation status</rdfs:label>
    </owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002009 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002009">
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its uncertainty qualifier, indicating that the asserted relationship is uncertain or suspected.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Obsoleted 2026-10-20. Named the axis but not the scope. Because uncertainty (CLCO:0000081) applies at any scope, an assertion through this property discarded the scope. The axis is already carried by the value class, so attach uncertainty through the property that commits to a scope.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:consider>CLCO:0003000, CLCO:0003001, CLCO:0003002</oboInOwl:consider>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002009</oboInOwl:id>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
        <rdfs:label>obsolete has uncertainty status</rdfs:label>
    </owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002100 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002100">
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to a qualifier that modifies a participant (subject or object) rather than the overall statement or predicate.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Obsoleted 2026-10-20. Its domain is the clinical association, so an assertion through this property could not say which participant was qualified, while the definitions of negation and uncertainty both promise a specific subject or object. Split into has subject qualifier (CLCO:0003001) and has object qualifier (CLCO:0003002).</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:consider>CLCO:0003001, CLCO:0003002</oboInOwl:consider>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002100</oboInOwl:id>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
        <rdfs:label>obsolete has participant qualifier</rdfs:label>
    </owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002500 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002500">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#topObjectProperty"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to a clinical entity that participates in it as either the subject or the object.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Analogous to RO:0000057 (has participant) but not declared as a sub-property because RO:0000057 relates a process (occurrent) to a continuant, whereas CLCO clinical associations are reified information content entities (subClassOf IAO:0000030), not processes. CLCO uses a statement reification pattern (n-ary relation) in which subject and object slots connect an association class to the entities it asserts a relationship between. This pattern has no direct equivalent in RO, which models relations as direct object properties between BFO-typed instances.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002500</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has association participant</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES: "The patient has pneumonia", "The MRI showed a mass", "Surgery was recommended" all contain clinical association participants.
NEGATIVE EXAMPLES: "patient" alone, "pneumonia" alone, and "quickly" do not assert an association participant.
CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES: In "The patient has pneumonia", the association has participants patient and pneumonia; in "The MRI showed a mass", it has MRI and mass; in "Surgery was recommended", it has surgery.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002500"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association (an asserted statement) to the entity that occupies the object position in that association.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>No direct RO equivalent exists. This property fills the object slot in a reified clinical statement (n-ary relation pattern). RO models relations as direct object properties, not as reified statements with named participant slots. See parent property CLCO:0002500 for further rationale.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002501</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has object</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES: In "The patient has pneumonia", pneumonia is the object; in "The patient received antibiotics", antibiotics is the object; in "The pain is severe", severe is the object.
NEGATIVE EXAMPLES: patient, received, and the are respectively subject, predicate, and determiner rather than objects.
CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES: Record OBJECT pneumonia for "The patient has pneumonia", OBJECT antibiotics for "The patient received antibiotics", and OBJECT severe for "The pain is severe".</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002500"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association (an asserted statement) to the entity that occupies the subject position in that association.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>No direct RO equivalent exists. This property fills the subject slot in a reified clinical statement (n-ary relation pattern). RO models relations as direct object properties, not as reified statements with named participant slots. See parent property CLCO:0002500 for further rationale.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002502</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has subject</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES: In "The patient has pneumonia", patient is the subject; in "The MRI showed a mass", MRI is the subject; in "The pain is severe", pain is the subject.
NEGATIVE EXAMPLES: pneumonia, mass, and severe are objects in those statements, not subjects.
CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES: Record SUBJECT patient, MRI, and pain for the three corresponding statements above.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002603 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002603">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000082"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its temporal frame value (historical, current, future, or hypothetical) indicating when the association holds relative to the clinical document.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Not a sub-property of RO:0002222 (temporally related to) because RO temporal relations use Allen interval algebra to order two occurrents (processes) relative to each other, whereas CLCO temporal frame is a categorical document-relative qualifier (historical/current/future/hypothetical) attached to a reified information entity. The RO_0001900 (temporal interpretation) annotation is retained to indicate that this property has temporal semantics.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <obo:RO_0001900 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001901"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>biolink:temporal_context_qualifier</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002603</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has temporal frame</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002222"/>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"The patient had pneumonia last year."
SOURCE: "pneumonia" =&gt; finding
TARGET: "last year" =&gt; temporal frame
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has temporal frame =&gt; TARGET

"The patient will undergo surgery tomorrow."
SOURCE: "surgery" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "tomorrow" =&gt; temporal frame
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has temporal frame =&gt; TARGET

NEGATIVE EXAMPLE:

"The patient has had headaches for three weeks."
"three weeks" describes duration, NOT the document-relative temporal frame.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002604 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002604">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000063"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to its workflow completion status (pending, in_progress, or completed). Orthogonal to temporal frame and intention.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Not a sub-property of has_temporal_context because completion status is not a temporal concept. A pending process may be temporally past (labs drawn yesterday, results pending).</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <obo:RO_0001900 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001901"/>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002604</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has completion status</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"The CT results are pending."
SOURCE: "CT results" =&gt; finding
TARGET: "pending" =&gt; completion status
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has completion status =&gt; TARGET

"The biopsy was completed."
SOURCE: "biopsy" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "completed" =&gt; completion status
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has completion status =&gt; TARGET

NEGATIVE EXAMPLE:

"The patient will undergo a biopsy."
"will undergo" indicates intention or future temporal frame, NOT completion status.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003000">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002000"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000067"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to a qualifier that modifies the entire assertion rather than one of its participants, such as intention, recommendation, or completion status.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Renamed from "has statement qualifier" on 2026-10-20. A clinical association is the reified statement, so statement scope and association scope are the same scope; the two properties were axiomatically identical and have been collapsed into this one. The label "has association qualifier" was carried by CLCO:0002000 until 2026-10-20; label-based lookups from before that date resolve to the grouping property, not to this one.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0003000</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has association qualifier</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLE:

"The physician recommended a biopsy."
SOURCE: "biopsy" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "recommended" =&gt; recommended
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has association qualifier =&gt; TARGET

"The patient intends to begin physical therapy."
SOURCE: "physical therapy" =&gt; intervention
TARGET: "intends" =&gt; intention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has association qualifier =&gt; TARGET

"The CT results are pending."
SOURCE: "CT results" =&gt; finding
TARGET: "pending" =&gt; pending
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has association qualifier =&gt; TARGET

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:

"The patient's mother has breast cancer."
Use: has subject qualifier or has object qualifier for a qualifier modifying a participant, NOT has association qualifier.

"The patient reported pain in his left knee."
Use: has subject qualifier or has object qualifier for a qualifier modifying a participant, NOT has association qualifier.

"The patient has had headaches for three weeks."
Use: has temporal frame or a duration representation as appropriate, NOT has association qualifier for the time expression alone.

Use a specific subproperty when one exists, such as has intention, has recommended, or has completion status. These four axes apply only to the whole assertion. For negation and uncertainty, which may apply at any scope, choose has association qualifier, has subject qualifier or has object qualifier according to what the qualifier modifies.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003001 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003001">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002000"/>
        <rdfs:range>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000080"/>
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000081"/>
                </owl:unionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </rdfs:range>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to a qualifier that modifies the entity filling its has subject slot, rather than the assertion as a whole or the entity filling its has object slot.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Added 2026-10-20, replacing has participant qualifier (CLCO:0002100), whose domain is the association and which therefore could not say which participant was qualified. Range is restricted to negation (CLCO:0000080) and uncertainty (CLCO:0000081), the two axes whose definitions state that they apply at any scope. Follows the same convention as SNOMED CT "Subject relationship context" and the Biolink subject_/object_ qualifier slots, which both identify the participant role in the attribute name.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0003001</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has subject qualifier</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"Possible pneumonia was treated with antibiotics."
SOURCE: "pneumonia" =&gt; finding
TARGET: "Possible" =&gt; uncertainty
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has subject qualifier =&gt; TARGET
The treatment did happen; only the diagnosis is uncertain. Attaching the uncertainty to the whole
statement would wrongly say the treatment may not have happened.

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:

"The patient was possibly treated with antibiotics elsewhere."
Use: has association qualifier. Here the treatment itself is what is uncertain.

"The patient will start chemotherapy."
Use: has intention. Intention, recommended and completion status are statement-only axes and are
outside the range of this property.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003002 -->

    <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0003002">
        <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002000"/>
        <rdfs:range>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000080"/>
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000081"/>
                </owl:unionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </rdfs:range>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Relates a clinical association to a qualifier that modifies the entity filling its has object slot, rather than the assertion as a whole or the entity filling its has subject slot.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Added 2026-10-20, replacing has participant qualifier (CLCO:0002100), whose domain is the association and which therefore could not say which participant was qualified. Range is restricted to negation (CLCO:0000080) and uncertainty (CLCO:0000081), the two axes whose definitions state that they apply at any scope. Follows the same convention as SNOMED CT "Subject relationship context" and the Biolink subject_/object_ qualifier slots, which both identify the participant role in the attribute name.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0003002</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has object qualifier</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:

"Tenderness is present, but not in the right upper quadrant."
SOURCE: "tenderness" =&gt; finding
TARGET: "not" =&gt; negation
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has object qualifier =&gt; TARGET
The finding is asserted; only the site is negated.

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:

"The patient denied having a fever."
Use: has association qualifier. The whole assertion is negated, not one participant.

"The biopsy was completed."
Use: has completion status. Completion status is a statement-only axis and is outside the range of
this property.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:ObjectProperty>
    


    <!-- 
    ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    //
    // Data properties
    //
    ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
     -->

    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002700 -->

    <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002700">
        <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The point in time at which the clinical association begins to hold.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Complements (does not replace) the reified temporal association has_onset (CLCO_0000022). Use this data property for computable timestamps from structured EHR data or normalized NLP output; use has_onset when the temporal expression itself is extracted from narrative text and needs its own qualifiers.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002700</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has start time</rdfs:label>
    </owl:DatatypeProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002701 -->

    <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002701">
        <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The point in time at which the clinical association ceases to hold.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002701</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has end time</rdfs:label>
    </owl:DatatypeProperty>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002702 -->

    <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002702">
        <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#duration"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The ISO 8601 duration for which the clinical association holds.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Complements (does not replace) the reified temporal association has_duration (CLCO_0000053). Use this data property for computable duration values; use has_duration when the duration expression needs its own qualifiers.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0002702</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has duration value</rdfs:label>
    </owl:DatatypeProperty>
    


    <!-- 
    ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    //
    // Classes
    //
    ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
     -->

    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000001">
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An entity is anything that exists or has existed or will exist.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>entity</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES: patient, pneumonia, and MRI are entities.
NEGATIVE EXAMPLES: quickly, because, and was are not entities.
CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES: In "The patient has pneumonia", patient and pneumonia are entities; in "The MRI showed a mass", MRI and mass are entities.</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000058"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000110"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Destination of patient transference</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>LOINC:LP203270-6</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>OMOP_CONCEPT_ID:1014654</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000002</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>is transferred to</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"transferred to the ICU" =&gt; is transferred to
"sent to the rehabilitation unit" =&gt; is transferred to
"moved to the operating room" =&gt; is transferred to

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"admitted to the ICU" =&gt; is admitted, NOT is transferred to
"referred to cardiology" =&gt; is referred by, NOT is transferred to
"discharged home" =&gt; is discharged, NOT is transferred to

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The patient was transferred to the ICU."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "ICU" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is transferred to =&gt; TARGET
"She was sent to the rehabilitation unit."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "rehabilitation unit" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is transferred to =&gt; TARGET
"He was moved to the operating room."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "operating room" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is transferred to =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A clinical process related to tracking administrative responsibilitity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Do not instantiate.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000003</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>clinical administration</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"admitted to the hospital" =&gt; clinical administration
"referred to cardiology" =&gt; clinical administration
"discharged home" =&gt; clinical administration

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"chest pain" =&gt; finding, NOT clinical administration
"MRI" =&gt; intervention, NOT clinical administration
"recommended surgery" =&gt; clinical decision, NOT clinical administration

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The patient was admitted to the hospital."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "admitted to the hospital" =&gt; clinical administration
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical administration =&gt; TARGET
"She was referred to cardiology."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "referred to cardiology" =&gt; clinical administration
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical administration =&gt; TARGET
"He was discharged home."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "discharged home" =&gt; clinical administration
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical administration =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000005">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A clinical association representing a decision made during patient care, such as a treatment plan, referral, or diagnostic strategy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000005</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>clinical decision</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"decided to start chemotherapy" =&gt; clinical decision
"plan for surgical repair" =&gt; clinical decision
"decision to order an MRI" =&gt; clinical decision

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"chest pain" =&gt; finding, NOT clinical decision
"MRI" =&gt; intervention, NOT clinical decision
"admitted to the hospital" =&gt; clinical administration, NOT clinical decision

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The team decided to start chemotherapy."
SOURCE: "team" =&gt; clinician; TARGET: "start chemotherapy" =&gt; intervention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical decision =&gt; TARGET
"The plan was for surgical repair."
SOURCE: "plan" =&gt; clinical decision; TARGET: "surgical repair" =&gt; intervention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical decision =&gt; TARGET
"The physician decided to order an MRI."
SOURCE: "physician" =&gt; clinician; TARGET: "order an MRI" =&gt; intervention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical decision =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000057"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A clinical reason explaining the reasoning behind a treatment, intervention, condition or medical statement.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000006</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>clinical explanation</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"because of persistent pain" =&gt; clinical explanation
"due to concern for infection" =&gt; clinical explanation
"to reduce the risk of recurrence" =&gt; clinical explanation

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"chest pain" =&gt; finding, NOT clinical explanation
"MRI" =&gt; intervention, NOT clinical explanation
"physician" =&gt; clinician, NOT clinical explanation

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The MRI was ordered because of persistent pain."
SOURCE: "MRI" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "persistent pain" =&gt; reason
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical explanation =&gt; TARGET
"Antibiotics were started due to concern for infection."
SOURCE: "antibiotics" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "concern for infection" =&gt; reason
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical explanation =&gt; TARGET
"Surgery was performed to reduce the risk of recurrence."
SOURCE: "surgery" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "reduce the risk of recurrence" =&gt; reason
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; clinical explanation =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom>
                    <owl:Class>
                        <owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                            <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000102"/>
                            <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000109"/>
                        </owl:unionOf>
                    </owl:Class>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000108"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>upload OSH CT chest pulmonary vein protocol</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A member of the clinical team has a specific action</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>OMOP_CONCEPT_ID:4044702</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMED:129450000</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000007</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>has action</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"scheduled the procedure" =&gt; has action
"uploaded the record" =&gt; has action
"called the patient" =&gt; has action

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"chest pain" =&gt; finding, NOT has action
"MRI" =&gt; intervention, NOT has action
"physician" =&gt; clinician, NOT has action

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The nurse scheduled the procedure."
SOURCE: "nurse" =&gt; clinician; TARGET: "scheduled the procedure" =&gt; task
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has action =&gt; TARGET
"Staff uploaded the record."
SOURCE: "staff" =&gt; clinician; TARGET: "uploaded the record" =&gt; task
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has action =&gt; TARGET
"The office called the patient."
SOURCE: "office" =&gt; setting; TARGET: "called the patient" =&gt; task
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; has action =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000058"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000110"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Patient admitted to ICU</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Statement indicating that the patient was admitted to a specific setting</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Does not exist in OMOP, admission is specific for inpatients or other facility types.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000010</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>is admitted</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"admitted to the hospital" =&gt; is admitted
"admitted to the ICU" =&gt; is admitted
"admitted for observation" =&gt; is admitted

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"transferred to the ICU" =&gt; is transferred to, NOT is admitted
"discharged home" =&gt; is discharged, NOT is admitted
"referred to cardiology" =&gt; is referred by, NOT is admitted

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The patient was admitted to the hospital."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "hospital" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is admitted =&gt; TARGET
"She was admitted to the ICU."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "ICU" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is admitted =&gt; TARGET
"He was admitted for observation."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "observation" =&gt; setting
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is admitted =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000006"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom>
                    <owl:Class>
                        <owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                            <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000101"/>
                            <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000102"/>
                        </owl:unionOf>
                    </owl:Class>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000102"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Here for packing and splint removal after recent discharge from rehab</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Statement indicating that an intervention or finding precedes an intervention or finding.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Used to be "is followed by" but this causes confusion when following refers to a particular clilnician responsible for a patient's care</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>OMOP_CONCEPT_ID:4272244</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMED:367409002</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000011</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>precedes</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"preceded by" =&gt; precedes
"before" =&gt; precedes
"followed by" =&gt; precedes

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"because of" =&gt; has reason, NOT precedes
"caused by" =&gt; is caused by, NOT precedes
"recommended" =&gt; recommended, NOT precedes

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The biopsy preceded the surgery."
SOURCE: "biopsy" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "surgery" =&gt; intervention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; precedes =&gt; TARGET
"The fever occurred before the rash."
SOURCE: "fever" =&gt; finding; TARGET: "rash" =&gt; finding
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; precedes =&gt; TARGET
"The follow-up visit followed hospital discharge."
SOURCE: "hospital discharge" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "follow-up visit" =&gt; intervention
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; precedes =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000012">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000108"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000102"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>A barium swallow was performed by Dr. PHI which was concerning for moderate aspiration.  Additionally, a hard palate lesion was seen.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Report of provider performing an intervention or assessment</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>There is OMOP 4160030, but it is a more general type of performed.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000012</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>is performed by</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"performed by Dr. Smith" =&gt; is performed by
"administered by the nurse" =&gt; is performed by
"read by the radiologist" =&gt; is performed by

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"referred by Dr. Smith" =&gt; is referred by, NOT is performed by
"seen by the cardiologist" =&gt; is seen by, NOT is performed by
"recommended by the physician" =&gt; recommended, NOT is performed by

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The barium swallow was performed by Dr. Smith."
SOURCE: "barium swallow" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "Dr. Smith" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is performed by =&gt; TARGET
"The medication was administered by the nurse."
SOURCE: "medication" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "nurse" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is performed by =&gt; TARGET
"The scan was read by the radiologist."
SOURCE: "scan" =&gt; intervention; TARGET: "radiologist" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is performed by =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000013">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000108"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000110"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PHI is referred by Dr.  PHI  with PMH significant for IP</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Statement indicating that the patient has been referred by a clinician</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>OMOP_CONCEPT_ID:44792349</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMED:342701000000109</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000013</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>is referred by</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"referred by her primary care physician" =&gt; is referred by
"sent by the emergency physician" =&gt; is referred by
"referred by the oncologist" =&gt; is referred by

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"performed by Dr. Smith" =&gt; is performed by, NOT is referred by
"seen by the cardiologist" =&gt; is seen by, NOT is referred by
"recommended by the physician" =&gt; recommended, NOT is referred by

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The patient was referred by her primary care physician."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "primary care physician" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is referred by =&gt; TARGET
"He was sent by the emergency physician."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "emergency physician" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is referred by =&gt; TARGET
"She was referred by the oncologist."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "oncologist" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is referred by =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000014">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000003"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002501"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000108"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0002502"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000110"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>[Speaker 2]: I went to the ENT at PHI and they prescribed Allegra D, and I took those, they cleared it up, but they also affected my blood pressure</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Statement indicating that the patient has seen a clinician</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>Mapping to seen by health care giver which I think is the intention here.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>OMOP_CONCEPT_ID:4091832</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMED:281033004</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>CLCO:0000014</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>is seen by</rdfs:label>
    <obo:IAO_0000112>POSITIVE EXAMPLES:
"seen by cardiology" =&gt; is seen by
"evaluated by the surgeon" =&gt; is seen by
"followed by a neurologist" =&gt; is seen by

NEGATIVE EXAMPLES:
"referred by Dr. Smith" =&gt; is referred by, NOT is seen by
"performed by Dr. Smith" =&gt; is performed by, NOT is seen by
"recommended by the physician" =&gt; recommended, NOT is seen by

CONTEXTUAL EXAMPLES:
"The patient was seen by cardiology."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "cardiology" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is seen by =&gt; TARGET
"She was evaluated by the surgeon."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "surgeon" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is seen by =&gt; TARGET
"He is followed by a neurologist."
SOURCE: "patient" =&gt; patient; TARGET: "neurologist" =&gt; clinician
RELATION: SOURCE =&gt; is seen by =&gt; TARGET</obo:IAO_0000112></owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CLCO_0000015">
